f the  trench sadly this little bird is in  decline the period between 1972 and 1996  saw the loss of 75 percent of the UK  breeding population changes to farming  practices have seen a lack of suitable  nesting habitat allowing the birds only  a small chance of raising a successful  brood of chicks some farms are now  creating suitable habitats by leaving  unseeded areas of 5 to 10 meters around  the centre of arable fields preferring  short vegetation and areas of grasses in  which it can forage for food the Skylark  finds refuge in the quiet areas of our  country parks many of the district's  open spaces provide this habitat and  enable the birds to have a better chance  of successful breeding male birds can  climb as high as a hundred metres above  the fields singing initially to attract  a mate then after to defend his  territory the song will last for a few  minutes but later in the season this can  exceed 20 minutes being a ground nesting  bird both male and female will sing to  attract attention away  for the nest running along the ground  first before taking flight singing  defiantly when predators approach three  to six eggs are laid in June and a  second or third brood may be started  later in the year the eggs are colored  yellow and white with brownish purple  spots at the large end a good mimic the  Skylark will often include within its  own song the cause of other birds that  share the local area so when you're out  next take a moment lie back enjoy the  larks sweet summer song and remember  boosting business growth the first  business summit has been held by  Rochford District Council to pick start  plans for economic growth in the area  the event was hosted by the council's  economic development team which provides  local businesses with ongoing support  and advice attendees had the chance to  shape the economic growth strategy that  will drive forward investment  opportunities over the next three years  Sean scrutton Rochford District  Council's he